---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Single-location dental practice, Veneto — over 30 years in business' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Positioning in 2024–2025, CRM-driven campaign in 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Positioning strategy design; end-to-end management of campaign, CRM and automations' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Meta Ads (primary), Google Ads (legacy)' },
  { label: 'Tools', value: 'CRM with pipeline, WhatsApp automations, shared tracking sheets' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '+600%', label: 'service line revenue in one year' },
  { metric: '46.5%', label: 'close rate (up from ~18-20% before)' },
  { metric: '~17.7×', label: '2026 campaign ROAS — mid-way figure' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="From a complex diagnosis to measurable revenue: positioning and automation in a single strategy"
  description="Case study: a proprietary clinical-communication protocol and a campaign with CRM and WhatsApp automation. Close rate more than doubled."
  eyebrow="Positioning · CRM · Automation"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>
    A long-established practice with a treatment line — periodontology — that's clinically solid
    but a hard sell: patients don't feel the urgency, the diagnosis is complicated to explain, and
    competitors push easier solutions instead. Right next to it, an implantology line with the
    opposite problem: demand was clear, but leads kept slipping through the cracks during
    follow-up.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Two challenges, side by side: build credibility and conversion into the periodontal diagnosis,
    and build a system that follows every implantology lead all the way to a decision — without
    losing a single one along the way.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    I designed the positioning strategy from the ground up: a proprietary clinical-communication
    protocol that turns the diagnosis into a clear, objective four-stage journey — microbiological
    tests, laser technology, follow-up checks — backed by dedicated materials. On the 2026
    implantology campaign I ran the whole chain myself: campaign structure, a CRM pipeline shared
    with the front desk, WhatsApp automations, and nurturing flows for leads who weren't ready
    yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    The insight behind the protocol: the problem was never convincing the patient to get treated.
    It was making them <em>see</em> the disease. Turn the diagnosis into something objective —
    specific tests, clearly explained — and the conversation shifts from price to health on its
    own. The numbers backed it up: close rate more than doubled in a year.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    A note on method: the periodontal line's growth is the product of the whole strategy —
    communication, materials, in-practice training, word of mouth — not one campaign that can be
    isolated. The correlation with the protocol's launch is strong, but I can't break down the
    exact contribution of each factor with the data I have. Better to say so.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution</h2>
  <p>
    For the positioning: a four-stage diagnostic journey, informational materials compliant with
    healthcare communication rules, and in-practice training on how to explain the protocol. For
    the 2026 campaign: a CRM pipeline with explicit stages, meticulous tracking of every contact
    alongside the front desk, and WhatsApp automations built to keep working, over time, on leads
    stuck at follow-up — the exact stage where, without automation, the most value leaks away.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    The periodontal line went from around 30 diagnosed patients and 5-6 closed treatments in 2024
    to 114 diagnosed and 53 closed in 2025 — close rate up from ~18-20% to 46.5%, and revenue from
    that treatment alone up from around €17,000 to over €120,000. The 2026 implantology campaign,
    at its midpoint, had already generated 33 leads and 4 patients for €31,680 in revenue on
    €1,785.78 in spend: a partial ROAS of around 17.7×, with 6 more opportunities still moving
    through the nurturing flows.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    Positioning and automation aren't two separate jobs. One makes the patient receptive; the
    other makes sure that receptiveness doesn't go to waste. And treating the diagnosis as a
    moment of value — not a box to tick on the way to a quote — changes patient behavior more than
    any campaign optimization ever could.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
